A||||16||zeusrekning||2.8, 3.2, 3.4, What are the differences other than cid'||||5||
Z||000000||zeusrekning||05-08-2000||10:20 PM||||Are the blocks the same. will they bolt up to the stock fiero trans? I want to get another fiero but dont think i'll go with the v8. what is the easiest (ease to power combo). What can of HP can be expected from each? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||05-08-2000||10:47 PM||||External diminsions are the same. Different years are balanced differently- pre '88's were externally balanced with the flywheel, 88 and later are internally balanced. All the engines you mentioned are 60 degree engines and share many components- heads, oil pans, camshafts etc... Dont forget about the 3.8 Buick v6's. They are also 60 degree engines and have big power potential, but have fun finding a manual tranny flywheel!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||05-08-2000||11:03 PM||||Everything is based on the 2.8.<BR>A 3.1/3.2 is a 2.8 with different crankshaft and piston rods. You can convert a 2.8 to a 3.1 by just swapping the crank and rods. A 3.2 is cylinders bored .030 with new pistons installed. A 3.4 is a 3.1 with a bigger bore to make it a 3.4. You cannot bore out a 2.8 to a 3.4 due to the blocks internal construction But they are physically the same size on the outside.<BR> The 2.8's have average 130hp GM advertised 140 for 85/86 and 135 for 87/88. A 3.1/3.2 typically sits around 150-170 depending on cam, valves compression, etc. A GM built 3.1 was advertised at 150hp. the 3.4 runs 160 to 200 depending on what goes in it. GM built 3.4's are 160 for the normal engine and 180 for the Ram Air engine in Grand Am's. The 3.4 does require the opposite side of the block to be drilled for the starter. Some people have a guide for this. There are some small items that need to change too. Example is for a 3.4 you need an adjustable pressure regulator If you use the Fiero intake system or the computer if you use the intake system from a new car. Of course your horsepower will vary depending on what you put in. As for max HP from a normally aspirated view, I've seen 2.8's reach around 160 and 3.1/3.2 reach around 175. The 3.4 should be able to reach 210. For more HP from any of the above that involves turbos. Super chargers, or NOS. The above engines use the Fiero mounts and is a direct bolt-on for the transmissions. For reliable HP over 200 hp it is probably better to go to a 3.8L, 3800SC or a V-8. Reliability being the key point. What it would cost to build the smaller V-6 you could go to the larger engines. Of course this is just my opinion. <P>Speed is just a question of money.....<BR>....How fast do you want to go?||||reg||2
Z||000003||zeusrekning||05-08-2000||11:30 PM||||Oreif,<BR>You are the man. Personaly I think my 4tech has power so i think th 2.8 with a rebuild kit and new cam would be great for me. would using the stroker kit on the 2.8 affect the reliability any? I want something i can drive cross country in and get there quick. <BR>Not looking for a 400 hp v6 or anything. Just want a reliable fiero so i can tear my 4banger apart.||||reg||10
Z||000004||cobra427||05-09-2000||12:26 AM||||Zeus,<BR>Just to correct a couple of errors in the previous post, a 3.1 is achieved by using  the crank and pistons and not the crank and rods. Also a buick 3.8 is not a 60* engine but rather a 90* engine. Some say that I'm a little picky but thos are the facts.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phaeton||05-09-2000||02:59 AM||||Little picky! Little picky!<P>Putting in a 3.1 stroker doesn't hurt reliability unless a lot of other mods are made at the same time.||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||05-09-2000||05:41 AM||||I would have to disagree about the need for a fuel regulator being a necessity on a 3.4. If you are using the 3.4 fuel rail/injectors there won't be any problems. We usually have the injectors cleaned and balanced when the engine is being set up. Never had a problem. ||||reg||6
Z||000007||Oreif||05-09-2000||10:03 AM||||WKDFIRO ~ Can you use the 3.4L fuel system with the Fiero ECM? I've heard that using a different intake system requires a different ECM. I do know if you use the Fiero intake system it is recommended to up the fuel pressure. But if the stock 3.4 intake works with Fiero ECM that's great! Also does the 3.4 fuel system use the "disc" type injectors simular to the 3.1L?<P>Cobra ~ In my response the 3.1L is the original GM built 3.1L. The 1989 Grand Prix service manual shows the crank measurements and rod measurements different than a 2.8L but the piston bore size is the same.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric||05-09-2000||10:12 AM||||I think the Grand Prix had aluminum heads.||||reg||1
Z||000009||WKDFIRO||05-09-2000||02:27 PM||||Are you referring to using the 3.4 intake as opposed to the Stock Fiero Intake? Here in California its just so much easier to have the smog guy look under the trunk lid and see a stock intake and assume that its a stock 2.8. <BR>    <BR>Using the stock intake with the stock ECM is no problem. The injectors I beleive are the disc type. Out of the 50 or so cars that came out of Fiero Motorsports with 3.4s NONE of them have had adjustable fuel regulators and NONE of them have had any fuel preasure problems.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cooter||05-09-2000||03:35 PM||||Oops- I meant the Buick V6 has the same bell housing mounting pattern as the 60 degree v6's. Sorry about that.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||05-09-2000||04:02 PM||||Thanks for the info, I have always heard varying opinions but never a definate answer. Now I know. I would like to go to a 3.4L or a V-8. The 3.4 switch would be less expensive and something I could do in the garage in a weekend. Of course there nothing like the sound of a V-8! But I don't have the $10,000 (or more) to do it. I figure a 3.4L conversion would be less than $2500 with me doing the labor.||||reg||1
